 to pay for this  insurance  the next thing that happens is an  insurer sends a message to the dap  saying yes yes i'm willing to accept  this policy  here's the 10 ethers that i guarantee  this policy with and i'm telling you  that i accepted the policy that the user  suggested  okay then june 1st comes along and now  we have to decide did it rain or did it  not rain well for that we use what's  called an oracle so in this case we'll  use the weather service as an oracle so  the dap basically when it was  established  it basically says i will accept the  weather  messages from the address of the weather  service uh to be authentic to tell to  tell me whether there was rain or not on  a particular day okay so the weather  service basically says sends a message  the message has no value  but the message says on june 1st in fact  there was no rain okay so now the dap  received a message from the oracle the  weather service  it knows it's authentic because the  message is signed using the weather  service secret key  and then the code in the dap said well  fine if  on june 1st there was no rain what i  need to do is i'm going to send  say 0.1 ether to the weather service to  compensate them for their service and  then i'm going to send the  insurance premium  plus i'm going to refund  the policy cost to the insurer yeah so  i'm going to send 10.9 ethers out to the  insurer  if it did rain on june 1st what the dap  would do is it would send the 0.9 ether  to the insurer and then 10 ethers to the  to the user who bought the policy  okay so that's a very simple  example of adapt just to show you why  people might want to send money to the  dap and why the app might need to  distribute those funds as it operates  now i want you to understand that these  dapps basically are capturing what we  would call business logic right very  simple business logic is captured here  and in fact all that programs basically  in one way or another  capture some form of business logic that  